course: dictionary
lesson: 6189
title: Mischief and tricks
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Mischief is naughty behaviour that is playful and not very harmful, like playing tricks on people. «The twins got up to mischief while Mum was on the phone.» The words around it tell you the twins were being cheeky, not cruel.
explain: Mischief can also mean trouble caused on purpose. «You must have meant some mischief, or else you'd have signed your name like an honest man.» Here the word hints that someone did something deliberately, so it is more serious than a simple joke.
explain: Mischief is an uncountable noun, so we say some mischief or full of mischief, not a mischief. It can also describe a look or a twinkle: «Twinkling eyes mean fun and mischief, so she is probably hiding a present.» Here it shows a playful wish to tease.
fact: Mischief is a noun, and it is uncountable: you say some mischief, not a mischief.
fact: «Mischief» means naughty, playful behaviour that is not very harmful, like playing tricks.
fact: «Mischief» can also mean harm or trouble caused on purpose.
fact: A look of mischief or a twinkle of mischief shows a wish to tease or play tricks.
fact: Useful phrases: get up to mischief, keep out of mischief, make mischief, full of mischief.
exercise: «The King decides that the Knave must have imitated someone's handwriting, and that not signing it proves he is up to mischief.» What does mischief mean here? | A tune played on a piano | Hard work in the garden | A big, tasty meal | *Trouble caused on purpose
exercise: The cat's eyes had a twinkle of mischief. What does this show? | Pain | Fear | *A wish to play a trick | Tiredness
exercise: Which sentence uses mischief correctly? | *She was full of mischief. | She was full of the mischiefs. | She was full of a mischief.
test: «The puppy grabbed a sock and ran off with a wag of its tail.» What is the puppy up to? | Serious danger | Feeling lonely on a long walk | Sleeping soundly | *Playful mischief
test: «Keep out of mischief while I make the tea.» What does mischief mean here? | A kind of bread | A loud song | *Naughty or harmful trouble | A long walk
exam: «He was full of mischief.» Which word could replace mischief? | Hunger | Sleep | Cold | *Tricks
